t :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5d1fe0-66e0-5072-aedc-5e9a7b18459a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "904dff05-ecb6-51b3-917d-26bcf8537c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-112615" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24578e-481e-553a-a83a-dc64b6635d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244416} {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb12f3-3780-53db-adc2-7c2e38923858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245004} {:answer/id #uuid "b902a4ce-fd1e-5700-be25-6073ff3196a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245966} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb9253d-1f88-5f8c-9b58-b795ed407dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-3)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae9a65-2c31-50dc-9b0c-6b6e3de50ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4055097-e27e-5747-8711-3ff850d11a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-112617" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292959fc-5935-574c-9f04-13e8671cf522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-7)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244674} {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} {:answer/id #uuid "45503d60-7034-5dc8-94fc-0693fe24418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbc7b91-18d2-5c35-b07b-b9f5fa63305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-x)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246129} {:answer/id #uuid "f620a82c-3ecf-57d2-9154-b09ad9b1016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c5333b-8831-529d-9a73-a9a967c8862b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8eab9f-7b10-5db7-a80e-51f13f149526" :question/math-academy-id "q-112626" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18490f60-4edc-508c-abe1-e7e0f13c065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285003} {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} {:answer/id #uuid "e405d079-ad9d-55f2-b5eb-d4d63e4f65f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb434cbb-a7e4-590f-89bc-63a9f59cf077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286942} {:answer/id #uuid "f66903c6-f94c-504b-bfea-93036d198d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecf3453-145b-5f3a-bf2b-36b631e0d173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78f6b62-d8f4-5963-b2aa-dcc684d0394b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18862" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039} :answer-field/id #uuid "65363172-90a7-5c3c-8285-eb6ba0e5a62c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c8e3cf5-7e90-5470-a693-ef40ffd528ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-328505" :question/problem "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263727d6-d29d-5bf3-b0ce-64f4c4babbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518549} {:answer/id #uuid "34dd07d8-0fb5-5860-b429-c5d95674c1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518551} {:answer/id #uuid "632410d0-6cb4-56f4-acbe-ce18db1f0810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518556} {:answer/id #uuid "9c538ed4-e63a-59ea-b5d7-1bf260ff251e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518566} {:answer/id #uuid "cf48ea9f-6f25-5c77-b923-06e53622bb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632410d0-6cb4-56f4-acbe-ce18db1f0810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518556} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1f5d50-c28c-5707-9506-4115f417afd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0419dbc0-1445-5bc9-b917-8b83592d2040" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4.$ We know that $(x - 2)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-14$ | $23$ | $-16$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $14$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4}{x - 2}=3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2}{x - 1}=3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)(3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 1)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118ff845-c36f-5dd4-b4be-4d2f3371534c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(3 - x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518547} {:answer/id #uuid "78bfce02-698c-5299-8a19-d1bf108af9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518558} {:answer/id #uuid "841dff1d-35b4-5aa5-8546-c8d913c38da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 8)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518559} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ae0968-bc81-5c09-a845-ff58e0c7403c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 - x)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518568} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6aa70-6d67-5a46-a185-c447bb5eb7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6aa70-6d67-5a46-a185-c447bb5eb7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518574} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4776a5-a31b-5b77-b1f4-5f3410bc9a46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518569}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc14d2b-da71-5a68-9167-1fb9cf53907c" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48.$ We know that $(x - 3)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-37$ | $82$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $15$ | $-66$ | $48$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48}{x - 3}=x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16}{x - 1}=x^{2} + 6 x - 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)(x^{2} + 6 x - 16).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(x - 2)(x + 8)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x - 2)(x + 8).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8f4cd9-343f-53f9-8da8-6987fb92aa94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518550} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5c0465-6f7c-51a9-b3cb-19ece0fb2e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518552} {:answer/id #uuid "5f60ea94-d78d-524d-860b-1089e09972ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518555} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b189d5-4fe1-5252-a78f-94a54754ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518577} {:answer/id #uuid "eef6d77e-1cb5-5f09-87d0-24953d96ba59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 - x)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b189d5-4fe1-5252-a78f-94a54754ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518577} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9a23eb-f99e-5776-9814-77b6f94072b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518561}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "996d568b-7577-52b3-ac06-045db9647695" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96.$ We know that $(x + 4)$ and $(x + 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x + 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x + 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x + 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $13$ | $62$ | $128$ | $96$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $-30$ | $-96$ | $-96$ |\n|  | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96}{x + 3}=x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 3)(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $-24$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32}{x + 4}=x^{2} + 6 x + 8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)(x^{2} + 6 x + 8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)\\underbrace{(x + 2)(x + 4)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x + 2)(x + 4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a850ce8-6c2f-56e9-97a6-d54695d622f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518553} {:answer/id #uuid "8f11b303-bb92-5db1-95e6-fc4409a201ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518562} {:answer/id #uuid "99dac682-5f1b-579c-8e61-9f4cda6acd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518565} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ee89c0-7563-5d3c-aabb-4556e6f4b071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518571} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e6fe5f-e80f-5c47-9d4c-f222856f886d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8e6fe5f-e80f-5c47-9d4c-f222856f886d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518572} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57ab9bd-7e2d-5b40-9052-da97505a6cff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518567}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de6b8b43-d602-5cd2-be14-bed17971288f" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6.$ We know that $(x - 1)$ and $(x - 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-7$ | $-13$ | $23$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6}{x - 1}=3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $9$ | $15$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6}{x - 3}=3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)(3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)\\underbrace{(3 x - 1)(x + 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 1)(x + 2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a93499-f229-5ff0-9ba6-3bde21bfc3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518545} {:answer/id #uuid "20c38d13-4072-56a0-9b5d-ac570ea86a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518548} {:answer/id #uuid "9901435b-c59a-5204-b93c-abef284df7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518563} {:answer/id #uuid "bec70bb8-e641-5a2a-909a-a7db4ae9cf80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518570} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8b82e6-c307-5894-ba37-88a78965716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c38d13-4072-56a0-9b5d-ac570ea86a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518548} :answer-field/id #uuid "872fae21-720c-5b0a-b5fa-d5ec9c29c2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518560}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e21b7f4a-96d6-5518-b504-9f9fc1a33098" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16.$ We know that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-17$ | $16$ | $20$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $12$ | $-20$ | $-16$ | $16$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16}{x - 4}=3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4}{x + 1}=3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)(3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 2).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083002 :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050a6387-6889-5322-b415-43b16c8ee200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235832} {:answer/id #uuid "080e2bdb-6bd9-5a54-8ed3-d64344f67f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235852} {:answer/id #uuid "a874fa44-6e99-5457-945a-4d9cd2c5b44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236871} {:answer/id #uuid "c3160ebf-88da-549e-9634-a3a8e9c0cb05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237049} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82572e-0c5c-5407-ab03-2ec87d72c77d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a874fa44-6e99-5457-945a-4d9cd2c5b44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd6f03e-b710-5161-8b1c-33472fec32b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c52b9d6-1dde-5a14-b98c-6e050ad50c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100495" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-2$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{-8}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f80ccbd-ccbc-5928-a550-6cad29085739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285384} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb6b034-8cbc-56de-af8d-76fb3ae60b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} {:answer/id #uuid "aa941969-e203-529e-83da-df1c431ff019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286344} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f188ae-0bb4-5125-90ce-5031c5e842ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286697} {:answer/id #uuid "daf267d2-0ede-5d02-9d21-9aac4c94a315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb6b034-8cbc-56de-af8d-76fb3ae60b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} :answer-field/id #uuid "44100842-ac04-513e-a491-fac5ffc3314e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273c340f-6b33-5250-84bf-da0aab3f9b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-20872" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1011c630-9a62-5590-95fe-1f85c1b5a80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284928} {:answer/id #uuid "5321b081-8102-5471-ac07-b8c310b99418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285553} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2408d1-f631-5b89-a568-5e893ae9f71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{224}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286362} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f6f6f9-f43c-5318-9566-01afbcfa0d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286584} {:answer/id #uuid "f643ae7f-0edc-5567-8838-69e01a836e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac2408d1-f631-5b89-a568-5e893ae9f71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{224}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286362} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f683c9c-a48c-57b6-bcfc-d829a293dda1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287281}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764c5e30-2e09-5150-97f0-e85e059f7437" :question/math-academy-id "q-20884" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=7x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=7x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{224}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d099e2e-ce29-59ef-a806-ace30618d5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273334} {:answer/id #uuid "96aa3414-8e59-55f9-9809-0762fe86875a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273691} {:answer/id #uuid "b859c76d-bcb6-56e0-843c-ba0b33580f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273985} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe75e26-c791-5ac9-b122-b37b44880890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274199} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e27676-13ee-5a89-959b-7e4ed36e3e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b859c76d-bcb6-56e0-843c-ba0b33580f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273985} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd837d8-0446-5745-b4ca-9695e2d529d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274801}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d17f43cd-3cd5-50eb-b926-556ea9fc28cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-152073" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{x}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bae842-8ea8-54bf-8caa-87bcb7235b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272698} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba29589-7ac1-5b15-9b66-4ade040f14e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272778} {:answer/id #uuid "59e7a905-086d-5ed4-9696-e6c68ab170ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273156} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77feb-7b20-5b17-bf9b-719ad3000622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273584} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1eba3a-d554-59b9-b5d3-7867821aee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23bae842-8ea8-54bf-8caa-87bcb7235b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272698} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d12876-0cf5-5965-9e9d-a67ed6348591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274748}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e55de38-da94-5dd6-b923-292738e6c28b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152077" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+2x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c687bd-090a-5ed1-98a6-47bea8132881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272738} {:answer/id #uuid "41fc8b25-2bac-59ac-9bbd-067dccd5f248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272949} {:answer/id #uuid "a749e3ee-0523-5ffb-addb-7fd36389c1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273836} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fa9fb0-7daf-5403-ab15-a4a8af85ebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274563} {:answer/id #uuid "f8778705-5831-548f-abf5-a0f772caa63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8778705-5831-548f-abf5-a0f772caa63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274567} :answer-field/id #uuid "827fdd34-4ca1-5b71-be17-2d801a4e5e06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "689b5589-3654-5d50-a22a-379b12d716b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152080" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-25$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}+2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-25}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-25}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-25)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{25}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaede482-c395-5fb3-a860-4bfc272f318c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaede482-c395-5fb3-a860-4bfc272f318c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319908} :answer-field/id #uuid "c17727e2-a8fa-5a86-a902-47b520f2bb3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a6b3e30-3fec-5d6f-b78c-1aea76522c56" :question/math-academy-id "q-350537" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9a0336-53d4-5bf4-982f-e1f8dd06d1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f9a0336-53d4-5bf4-982f-e1f8dd06d1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319868} :answer-field/id #uuid "484db524-b951-5719-aef0-f5c80cf2155c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320316}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45df630e-f651-5e97-a712-bb9ab8cb5834" :question/math-academy-id "q-350540" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(2x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(2x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f54055ec-36ba-5824-8218-234a554872b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54055ec-36ba-5824-8218-234a554872b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320127} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4712a85-84b6-549d-9780-1de6636a446e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "831e21bd-cf6a-5441-a556-4d450485c3a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-350541" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3y-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $y\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{32}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be5dbf1b-42c9-5026-9900-cfddb4a9ab09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5dbf1b-42c9-5026-9900-cfddb4a9ab09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319974} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b7901e-e348-574b-b81f-ea1a0bed468c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f50581dc-047f-51aa-9c85-54d3cfe920fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350542" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac32c37a-0545-5995-97eb-31abcfea4a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac32c37a-0545-5995-97eb-31abcfea4a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f622355a-a62e-5f2e-8bba-65498a7bc580" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320683}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f693e4-1a0c-5b3d-a5e5-3d444d6b2192" :question/math-academy-id "q-350543" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta -1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $\\theta \\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta -1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdcd8f9-3045-5dab-9637-fa2cd07c5924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fdcd8f9-3045-5dab-9637-fa2cd07c5924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319678} :answer-field/id #uuid "8434bcb6-d76e-5d61-9bb5-94b4cd6d2f12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "707c7ca6-19e8-5b59-9402-9dd2759606ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-350545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-3$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{20{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{4}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{27}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967f4588-0840-5021-ae5e-4b8befb7b9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "967f4588-0840-5021-ae5e-4b8befb7b9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319837} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e79540f-4225-5f79-b39e-1f940284579f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2431216c-f373-519f-8f53-e3942aed30af" :question/math-academy-id "q-350546" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-12$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-12}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-12)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76261153-11bb-5e8f-acff-a015b8e25166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369827} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1fe973-efd0-57a0-8e89-18f8b1c4d4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369828} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9ab8d5-091c-58b3-ab6c-c8c5102b4c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369829} {:answer/id #uuid "2624d772-082b-52d5-b55b-277f20787217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369830} {:answer/id #uuid "778e575d-c0dc-5c9e-a56f-8b35810b4320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2624d772-082b-52d5-b55b-277f20787217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369830} :answer-field/id #uuid "865b277f-3f6d-50c1-8588-7f86eda8ad8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369826}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f28297b2-fcdb-5764-82c4-855fe84621a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152079" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf43324-c63f-59fe-a56a-d4b9b0b7048c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369973} {:answer/id #uuid "d31fde31-428f-55b0-b1d5-e09807d05e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369974} {:answer/id #uuid "1e516990-d1af-5886-b235-bc549708f3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369975} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb22e6-abf7-597f-994c-d38d0bf975fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369976} {:answer/id #uuid "04436aad-8c02-5516-88b3-fa9120784970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31fde31-428f-55b0-b1d5-e09807d05e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369974} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbdfe1c-d664-50dc-a125-12450a2859e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14405449-2d85-5d75-b26c-28e2f262eedb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20869" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e4b664-17a3-5106-8fcc-1fd3157bcae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369980} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc1e7a0-2453-5614-9f5a-bc6ac50e35b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369981} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a6bf50-affa-568c-9968-8080ccebc668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369982} {:answer/id #uuid "991b1241-84cb-5f83-898f-d66765248890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369983} {:answer/id #uuid "350fcd55-16be-54a8-9da0-a999e2000ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bc1e7a0-2453-5614-9f5a-bc6ac50e35b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369981} :answer-field/id #uuid "088a38cb-9b1f-550f-8783-f07458bd8704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e107c6e8-f6e7-5be8-b0b1-dabc8fa3d89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20881" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{27}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{27}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341312de-df8d-50a0-8b5d-89acf6c49904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "341312de-df8d-50a0-8b5d-89acf6c49904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370635} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1b6083-72a1-5e77-9dc4-4a2062b1b215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85d6ec52-c42a-5131-ae64-adaa1af79927" :question/math-academy-id "q-350547" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{({x}^{7}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{7}-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=7{x}^{6}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{7}-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-4$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{({x}^{7}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494355} {:answer/id #uuid "16ccf152-39ad-58ab-b622-b32a7cb53d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494362} {:answer/id #uuid "34be0800-1b32-5b90-8444-e88edf6c9a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494423} {:answer/id #uuid "47fea95f-c75b-556a-9356-f42d7572185d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494462} {:answer/id #uuid "72662bf2-ec94-5cdc-8665-4b665b80f3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494355} :answer-field/id #uuid "46da6bbf-df72-58cf-ab47-22a431cba277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494948}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01e135d3-05cc-5121-8735-150ba42cbf5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118683" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}]}
